    <name>
      <text>Domain and Range of a Function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text>&lt;p&gt;We define the &lt;b&gt;real-valued&lt;/b&gt; function \( f(x)={@fx@} \)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Determine the domain and the range of \(f\), as subsets of the real numbers represented by inequalities.
&lt;br&gt;Also write these sets as intervals of the real numbers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Note: To enter intervals of you real line, you should write the relevant text in the table below.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;table width="250" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="0" border="1" style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;th style="text-align: center;"&gt;Intervals&lt;/th&gt;
    &lt;th style="text-align: center;"&gt;Input Syntax&lt;/th&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;\( [a,b] \)&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;code&gt;cc(a,b)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;\( (a,b) \)&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;code&gt;oo(a,b)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;\( [a,b) \)&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;code&gt;co(a,b)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;\( (a,b] \)&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;code&gt;oc(a,b)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
 &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;\( (a, \infty) \)&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;code&gt;oo(a,inf)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
 &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;\( (-\infty, b] \)&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;code&gt;oc(-inf,b)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
 &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;\( (-\infty, \infty) \)&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;code&gt;oo(-inf,inf)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;

&lt;br&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(a) \(Dom(f) = \{ x \in \mathbb{R} : \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] \(\leq x \leq\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] \(\}\) = [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[[feedback:prt1]]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(b) \(Range(f) = \{ y \in \mathbb{R} : \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] \(\leq x \leq\) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] \(\}\) = [[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[[feedback:prt2]]&lt;/p&gt;</text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text>&lt;h3&gt;Worked Solution&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(a) Since we require \(f\) to be real-valued, to determine the domain of \(f(x)\) we need to find the values of \(x\) for which \({@bb^2 - x^2@} \geq 0\).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To do this, we first find the roots of the quadratic. In this case,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt; \({@bb^2-x^2@} = {@(bb-x)*(bb+x)@}\)&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So, the roots are \(x = {@-bb@}\) and \(x = {@bb@}\).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now, since the coefficient of the \(x^2\) term is negative, the values between the roots will be positive. 
&lt;br&gt;In particular, we can verify that for \(x = 0\),&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\({@bb^2-x^2@} = {@bb^2@} &gt; 0\)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hence, the domain of \(f\) is the set \( \{ x \in \mathbb{R} : {@-bb@} \leq x \leq {@bb@} \}={@ta5@} \)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(b) To determine the range of \(f\), we need to find the minimal and maximal values of the function. Since \(f\) is continuous it must take every value between its minimum and maximum, so these values will give us the range.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To determine the minimum we have, by definition, that the square root function is non-negative. So,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\(f(x) \geq 0\).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To see that \(f\) does actually attain the value \(0\), we note that \(f({@bb@}) = 0\).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To determine the maximum, we note that the square root function is increasing, so \(f\) is maximised at the maximum of \({@bb^2@} - x^2\).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Since \(x^2 \geq 0\), we have,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-left: 30px"&gt;\(\begin{align*} 
    &amp; {@bb^2@} - x^2 \leq {@bb^2@} \\
    &amp;\Rightarrow f(x) = {@fx@} \leq {@bb@}
\end{align*}\)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To see that \(f\) does actually attain the value \({@bb@}\), we note that \(f(0) = {@bb@}\).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hence, we have noted that \(0 \leq f(x) \leq {@bb@}\) and \(f\) attains the values of \(0\) and {@bb@}.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Therefore, the range of \(f\) is the set \( \{ y \in \mathbb{R} : 0 \leq y \leq {@bb@} \}={@ta6@} \)&lt;/p&gt;</text>
    </generalfeedback>
